iency, while Lemma\u202f1 gives necessity. Hence the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line is either\n\n* vertical (\\(x=c\\)),\n* horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), or\n* of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, at least one vertical line and at least one horizontal line belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines and the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set of points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(1\\):\n\\[\nV:=\\{(1,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;1+b\\le n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\}.\n\\]\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) such points.  \n\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained **no** vertical line, then each point of \\(V\\) would have to be covered by a line whose slope is not \\(\\infty\\). Any non\u2011vertical line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, so distinct points of \\(V\\) would require distinct lines. That would demand at least \\(n\\) non\u2011vertical lines, contradicting \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\). Therefore a vertical line must be present; we may assume it is \\(x=1\\) (any vertical line intersecting \\(V\\) can be translated to \\(x=1\\) without affecting the argument).\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the set  \n\n\\[\nH:=\\{(a,1)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+1\\le n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nshows that \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain a horizontal line; we may take it to be \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus any admissible family contains at least one vertical and one horizontal line, i.e. at least two non\u2011sunny lines. Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction achieving any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Base non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Include  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lines of slope \\(-1\\).**  \n   For each integer  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   add  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{t}:\\;x+y=t .\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such lines; together with \\(L_1,L_2\\) we have \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   At this stage the family contains **no** sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)).\n\n3. **Turning some of them sunny.**  \n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, replace any \\(n-2-k\\) of the lines \\(L_{t}\\) by sunny lines as follows.  \n   For each chosen \\(t\\) pick a lattice point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) (such a point exists because \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)).  \n   Choose a slope \\(m\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\n   \\]\n\n   and define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{t}:\\; y-b = m\\,(x-a) .\n   \\]\n\n   The point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{t}\\), so the covering of that point is preserved.  \n   By selecting distinct slopes for different replaced values of \\(t\\) we guarantee that the new lines are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the already present vertical, horizontal and slope \\(-1\\) lines.\n\n   After performing this replacement for exactly \\(n-2-k\\) values of \\(t\\) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consisting of  \n\n   * the two fixed non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\