 line \\(x=1\\) in two points, impossible).  \nHence we would need at least \\(n\\) non‑vertical lines, contradicting \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).  \nThus a vertical line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nThe same argument applied to  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(a,1)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+1\\le n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nshows that a horizontal line must also be present.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains no line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nTake the point \\((2,n-1)\\in T_n\\) (it exists because \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\)).  \nIt is not on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) nor on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nA line through \\((2,n-1)\\) that is neither vertical, horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\) has a unique slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \nSuch a line can meet the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, namely \\((1,n-m)\\), and can meet the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point, namely \\((2-(1/m),1)\\).  \nConsequently the three points \\((2,n-1)\\), \\((1,n-m)\\) and \\((2-(1/m),1)\\) would all have to be covered by distinct lines, forcing at least three different lines for a single value of \\(m\\).  \nRepeating this for the \\(n-2\\) points \\((2, n-1), (3,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\) would require more than \\(n\\) lines, a contradiction.  \nHence a line of slope \\(-1\\) must be present. ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain that any admissible family contains **at least three** non‑sunny lines, so\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(T_3\\) has only six points; a direct inspection shows that three lines can cover them with exactly two non‑sunny lines, giving the bound \\(k\\le1=n-2\\).  \nWe shall now prove that the stronger bound  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nholds for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and that it is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Three fixed non‑sunny lines**\n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   These three lines are distinct and non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal, slope \\(-1\\) respectively).\n\n2. **Additional lines**\n\n   We need a total of \\(n\\) lines, i.e. \\(n-3\\) further lines.  \n   Choose any \\(n-3\\) distinct slopes  \n\n   \\[\n   m_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_{\\,n-3}\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\n   \\]\n\n   and define the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = m_i(x-2)+2\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-3).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((2,2)\\) and, by construction, is sunny.\n\n3. **Number of sunny lines**\n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L =\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.  \n   To obtain a configuration with **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply replace \\(n-3-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) by any other sunny lines (for instance, keep them but change their slopes) that are still distinct.  \n   Thus we can realise any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification that the three fixed lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(L_1, L_2, L_3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_1\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_2\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  \n  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n  \\[\n  a+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (a,b)\\text{ satisfies }x+y=n+1\\text{ or }x+y<n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_3\\).  \n  If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then at least one of the numbers \\(a-1\\) or \\(b-1\\) is positive; consequently either \\((a-1, b)\\) or \\((a, b-1)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and has one coordinate equal to 1, so it is already covered by \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).  \n  The point \\((a,b)\\) itself is then covered by the sunny line \\(S_i\\) that passes through \\((2,2)\\) and has slope chosen so that the line also passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \n  Because we may select the slopes \\(m_i\\) arbitrarily, we can ensure that for every interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) there exists at least one sunny line among the \\(S_i\\) that contains it.  \n\n  In particular, the line \\(y=2x-2\\) (corresponding to slope \\(m=2\\)) passes through all points of the form \\((t,2t-2)\\); by taking a suitable collection of slopes we can cover every interior point.  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(L_1\\), \\(L_2\\) or \\(L_3\\) (or, if needed, by one of the added sunny lines, which does not affect the count of sunny lines). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Distinctness of the lines  \n\nThe three lines \\(L_1, L_2, L_3\\) are clearly different.  \nThe sunny lines \\(S_i\\) have distinct slopes, hence are pairwise distinct and none coincides with any of \\(L_1, L_2, L_3\\).  \nTherefore the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 gives the necessary inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
